source: https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hyiZy6HdgWt9OfGpABK23pvsf4NxZo8w6GhAICOlFzE/edit
uses:
  - sourdough
  - poached-eggs
  - scrambled-eggs
  - fried-eggs
  - garlic-aioli
ingredients:
  - item: fresh sourdough slices
  - item: bacon
    note: chopped
  - item: eggs
    note: whisked, fried, or poached
  - item: feta cheese
    note: crumbled
  - item: tomatoes
    note: diced, or cherry tomatoes halved
  - item: spring onion
    note: sliced
  - item: silverbeet
    note: stems removed, leaves finely shredded; sub kale — see notes, it needs longer
  - item: fresh parsley
    note: finely chopped
  - item: fresh basil
    optional: true
    note: torn
  - item: olive oil
    optional: true
  - item: salt
    optional: true
  - item: pepper
    optional: true
components:
  - name: prep the silverbeet
    steps:
      - cut the tough white/coloured stems away from the leaves
      - wash the green leaves thoroughly to remove any grit
      - shred the leaves finely so they cook down quickly
      - 'stems: slice finely like celery and sauté 3 mins before the leaves go in — don''t waste them'
      - 'kale instead: strip and bin the ribs (they stay woody), shred the leaves finer than you would silverbeet'
  - name: crisp the bacon
    steps:
      - chopped bacon into a cold frying pan
      - heat to medium-high
      - cook until crispy and it has released its fat
      - remove with a slotted spoon, leaving the bacon fat in the pan
  - name: sauté the silverbeet & tomatoes
    steps:
      - silverbeet and tomatoes into the hot bacon fat
      - sauté 3-4 mins until the silverbeet is completely wilted and the tomatoes soften
      - 'kale instead: 5-7 mins, and add a splash of water or a knob of butter once it hits the fat — it won''t release liquid the way silverbeet does'
      - remove and set aside with the bacon
  - name: toast the sourdough
    steps:
      - wipe the pan lightly if needed, or use the remaining residue for flavour
      - drizzle the sourdough slices with olive oil
      - toast in the pan 2 mins each side until golden and crunchy
      - remove onto the serving plates
  - name: cook the eggs
    steps:
      - lower the heat to medium
      - 'option A (scramble): whisked eggs straight into the pan, stir gently until softly set'
      - 'option B (fried): crack straight into the pan, cook to your liking'
      - 'option C (poached): done separately and ahead — see poached-eggs'
  - name: assemble and garnish
    steps:
      - layer the wilted silverbeet and tomatoes onto the toasted sourdough
      - top with the eggs and crispy bacon
      - generously scatter the crumbled feta and sliced spring onion
      - finish with a heavy handful of fresh parsley
      - sauce or drizzle last, over the parsley
  - name: sauce & drizzle options
    ingredients:
      - item: balsamic glaze
        note: sweet, tangy contrast that cuts through the salty feta and rich bacon
      - item: chilli jam
        note: sweet heat, pairs with both the eggs and the silverbeet
      - item: garlic aioli
        note: rich and creamy, if you want to lean into comfort food
      - item: lemon olive oil
        note: fresh lemon juice whisked with extra virgin olive oil
    steps:
      - 'lemon olive oil: whisk lemon juice into extra virgin olive oil and drizzle over the parsley'
notes: |
  Prep 10 mins, cook 15 mins, one large frying pan. Everything comes out of the same pan in
  sequence and gets reassembled at the end — bacon fat is the through-line, so don't wash
  the pan between stages.

  The basil twist: if you use basil instead of parsley, tear the leaves rather than chopping
  them, and add them at the very last second — basil bruises easily and loses flavour if it
  gets too hot.

  Kale instead of silverbeet: works, but it isn't a straight swap. Silverbeet leaves are
  thin and mostly water — they collapse in 3-4 mins and release liquid that keeps the pan
  from catching. Kale is drier, waxier and more fibrous, so at the same timing you get
  chewy squeaky leaves and a dry pan, since there's nothing in there but bacon fat. Give it
  5-7 mins and a splash of water to steam it through. Strip and bin the ribs — the stem
  trick above only works because silverbeet stems are crisp and juicy; kale ribs stay woody.

  Cavolo nero over curly kale if you get the choice: flatter, more tender, sweeter, and it
  goes silky in a way curly kale doesn't. Curly works but leans crisp — not a bad dish at
  all, crispy kale against bacon, just a different one. Baby kale is near enough a drop-in
  and needs no extra time. Kale is more bitter and mineral than silverbeet, which the feta's
  salt and the lemon olive oil are already set up to handle.

  Poached is the upgrade over the doc's scramble/fried options. Do the 63°C bath the night
  before or while the bacon renders; the 2 min finishing poach slots in exactly where the
  doc has you cooking the eggs, and the yolk breaking over the sourdough is doing the same
  job a sauce would.

  Drink pairings (all under alcoholic/):
    bloody-mary        — mirrors the tomato and savoury notes of the dish
    grapefruit-paloma  — tequila, lime, grapefruit soda; sharp citrus cleanses the palate
                         after the rich bacon and cheese
    tom-collins        — gin, lemon juice, sugar syrup, soda; light, bubbly, hyper-refreshing
    aperol-spritz      — bright bitter orange cuts through the creamy feta

  Works as dinner as readily as breakfast.
